# Overlay for USB-C networking builds:
#   idf.py -D SDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS="sdkconfig.defaults;sdkconfig.usbnet" build
#
# The ESP32-S3's USB-Serial-JTAG and USB-OTG peripherals share the same two
# pins, so enabling TinyUSB necessarily removes the serial console. Logs are
# unavailable in this build -- use a console build for development.
CONFIG_VFO_USB_NET=y
CONFIG_TINYUSB_NET_MODE_NCM=y
# The console MUST be off in this build. USB-Serial-JTAG and USB-OTG share the
# same PHY, and leaving the console on it while installing TinyUSB crashed the
# device on every boot -- dmesg showed it re-enumerating as a JTAG/serial unit
# over and over, never as a network device.
#
# This does not make the device unflashable: the ROM bootloader brings up
# USB-Serial-JTAG on every reset regardless of app configuration, so ttyACM0
# always reappears for esptool. Holding a finger on the screen through boot
# additionally skips USB networking, so the port stays up after boot too.
CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_NONE=y
# CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_USB_SERIAL_JTAG is not set
# ESP_CONSOLE_NONE only disables the PRIMARY console. The SECONDARY console
# defaults to USB-Serial-JTAG, which sets ESP_CONSOLE_USB_SERIAL_JTAG_ENABLED
# and initialises that peripheral at startup -- so it keeps hold of the USB PHY
# that TinyUSB needs. Symptom: the device boots and runs perfectly stably, but
# never re-enumerates; the host keeps seeing 303a:1001 "USB JTAG/serial debug
# unit" and no network interface ever appears. Both consoles must be off.
CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_SECONDARY_NONE=y
# CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_SECONDARY_USB_SERIAL_JTAG is not set
# The USB link has nothing behind it, so its DHCP server must not name a DNS
# server either. With this on, every lease offered the knob itself as DNS, and
# a PC then sent name lookups down the cable that nothing would ever answer.
# CONFIG_LWIP_DHCPS_ADD_DNS is not set
# TLS without the AES hardware. Its DMA cannot read the TLS buffers, which are
# in PSRAM, so each record it decrypts borrows two 1.6 kB buffers of internal
# DMA memory -- and this build, with USB networking's ~14 kB of static RAM,
# has the least internal RAM of all. Mid-download, with WiFi's receive
# buffers holding the rest, that borrow failed ("esp-aes: Failed to allocate
# memory") and killed the download at 1-7%, every time; the firmware picker
# could not reach the setup firmware from this build. Software AES needs no
# buffers, keeps its tables in flash, and TCI is plain WebSocket: only the
# update downloads use TLS here.
CONFIG_MBEDTLS_HARDWARE_AES=n
